{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:rstar:fermi","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:93b303d62a2b26554392e87cf12b605b97a1819f9ecae437181659d86b044dd9","modelForecastIds":["sha256:9e32442dd80104ef31ec692efe35319d2d9fb5d07be418ab3e540ee18caa519a"],"modelUse":"consulted","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.7},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.7},{"probability":0.9,"value":2.8}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"GROWTH ACCOUNTING, BOTTOM-UP, PPP WEIGHTS RECONCILED FIRST. 2025 constant-PPP levels: OECD-38 ~ $75T (US ~30, euro-area OECD members ~24, Japan ~6.8, Korea ~3.3, UK ~4.5, Canada ~2.6, Mexico ~3.3, Turkiye ~3.6, rest ~ the remainder after overlap netting), China ~ $41T. Coupled base ~ $116T: w_OECD = 0.645, w_China = 0.355 in 2025. China grows faster, so by the 2031 base of the window w_OECD ~ 0.63, w_China ~ 0.37. (The sim adapter independently reports 2031 weights of 0.629 / 0.371 on its own nine-region proxy, which is a useful cross-check on the WEIGHT even though I reject its growth rates.)\nOECD-38 LEG = 1.20%/yr. (i) Locked 2A baseline: real GDP per capita 0.6%/yr \"absent productivity offsets,\" plus OECD-38 population growth ~+0.2%/yr (US + net migration carrying a shrinking European/Japanese/Korean core) = +0.80. (ii) Healthy-aging offset: the IMF global +0.4 pp/yr; I claim only half of it for the OECD and only because the OECD per-capita figure is explicitly stated as being BEFORE productivity offsets = +0.20. (iii) AI TFP lift, taken as the AI-Q6 regime expectation: 0.162(0.75) + 0.472(0.35) + 0.365(0.06) = 0.122 + 0.165 + 0.022 = +0.309, which I cut to +0.30 for my effort's own channel (a 10-13% nominal hurdle rate prunes the marginal long-duration project; see CQ4). (iv) Capital deepening from AI/grid capex: if the investment share rises +1.2 pp of GDP against K/Y ~3.2, extra K growth = 1.2/320 = 0.375%/yr; at alpha = 0.33 that is +0.12 pp, halved for short GPU asset lives (3-6 yr, so most of the spend is replacement not net accumulation) and for AI-Q4's 66% impairment marginal, and halved again for crowding-out of non-AI investment at a 2.0% real risk-free = +0.05. (v) Frictions: -0.05 climate damages at 1.72C (the model's own 2036 reading), -0.05 grid/mineral bottleneck, -0.05 debt-service crowd-out as net interest goes 2% -> 3% of GDP = -0.15. SUM: 0.80 + 0.20 + 0.30 + 0.05 - 0.15 = 1.20%/yr. Internal check by composition: US weight ~0.40 of OECD-38 PPP; 0.40(1.8) + 0.60(0.8) = 0.72 + 0.48 = 1.20. Consistent.\nCHINA LEG = 2.00%/yr, built twice and reconciled. Bottom-up: working-age headcount -1.1%/yr over 2032-36 (the 1962-72 cohort fully exits) times beta ~0.5 = -0.55, plus education/human-capital upgrading +0.3%/yr of effective labour at beta 0.5 = +0.15, net labour = -0.40. Capital: investment/GDP falling 41% -> ~35%, K/Y ~3.5, so gross K growth 0.35/3.5 = 10%/yr less ~6%/yr depreciation (short-lived property/infra) = ~4%/yr net K growth decelerating to ~3.5% by 2036; at alpha ~0.45, contribution = +1.60. TFP +0.70 (China leads industrial robot installs and has the strongest labour-scarcity automation pull in the coupled set, net of pension-subsidy and LGFV crowd-out). Frictions -0.30. Sum = -0.40 + 1.60 + 0.70 - 0.30 = 1.60%/yr. Top-down check against the lock: the locked -2.7 pp/yr China aging drag against a ~4.3%/yr no-aging convergence trend gives 1.60%/yr - the two methods agree exactly. I then move UP to 2.00% for one stated reason: the -2.7 pp is a 2025-2050 AVERAGE and the dependency shift is steeper after 2035 than before it, so the early-2030s drag is nearer -2.2 pp against a ~4.2% trend.\nTHE ARITHMETIC THE LENS ASKED FOR: China's PPP weight times its aging drag = 0.37 x (-2.2) = -0.81 pp of the coupled answer. OECD's per-capita loss = 0.63 x (-0.4) = -0.25 pp. Together the locked 2A block removes ~1.06 pp from a no-aging coupled baseline of ~2.56%/yr.\nCOUPLED P50 = 0.63(1.20) + 0.37(2.00) = 0.756 + 0.740 = 1.496, rounded to 1.5 and set at 1.7 to carry one asymmetry I believe in: the labour-scarcity DEMAND PULL for automation in physical sectors is understated in my TFP term and is the one channel where endogenous demographics help growth.\nSPREAD: sd ~0.82 pp on a 5-year average, so P10 = 1.7 - 1.282(0.82) = 0.65 -> 0.7 and P90 = 1.7 + 1.282(0.82) = 2.75 -> 2.8. P10 is a window containing one recession plus China at ~1%; P90 is the AI-Q6 breakout leg plus a Chinese consumption rebalancing.\nWHY I REJECT THE MODEL (adapter rule 3, mandatory). Model coupled 2032-36 growth: baseline 2.946%, central-path 2.945%, ai-energy-boom 3.031%, automation-boom 2.999%, tech-breakthrough 3.682%, net-zero 3.898%, regional-divergence 2.500%, tech-plateau 2.291%, tech-stagnation 2.299%, debt-populism 1.674%. My 1.7% equals only the model's WORST run. Four structural reasons, each a property of the code and not a taste: (1) the production function is Ayres-Warr with gamma = 0.55 on useful energy, a deliberate heterodox choice the repo itself flags against a mainstream cost-share gamma of 0.05-0.08, so fast electrification mechanically delivers GDP growth a cost-share function would not; (2) the capital module treats public debt interest as a tax-financed GDP claim and explicitly puts interest-capitalization spirals out of scope, so there is no fiscal-dominance growth channel at all - which is precisely my effort's channel; (3) the model has no price level and no unemployment, so no Baumol cost disease, no care-sector monopsony and no nominal rigidity can appear; (4) the efficiency/TFP engine is still delivering structural catch-up in the 2030s. On CHINA: the model's 3.81%/yr (baseline) vs my 2.00% is a 1.81 pp gap, almost exactly the size of the locked drag, confirming the adapter's point that the model does not implement it. Note the direction of the demographic conditioning: the model runs the UN WPP LOW variant, which should make its China number LOWER than a medium-variant number, not higher - so the disagreement is in the ECONOMICS, not the demography, which strengthens rather than weakens the case for discounting it. On DATACENTERS: the model's WORLD 2036 load is 1,884 TWh (baseline) to 4,180 TWh (ai-energy-boom) against AI-Q3's OECD-38+China P90 of 1,573.8 TWh; since world must exceed OECD-38+China, even the baseline is a strict inconsistency with the lock. The model's datacenter demand is braked only by an electricity-bill-share-of-GDP willingness-to-pay ceiling and by LCOE - there is no cost-of-capital brake on the shell/GPU capex and no financing-structure fragility, which is exactly the channel AI-Q4's 66% prices. I read the disagreement as evidence the model's load is too high, not that the lock's P90 is too low. ALL MODEL FIGURES ARE LOW-FERTILITY-CONDITIONAL (UN WPP low variant, ~18% below medium by 2100), and its region proxy is us + oecd-ex-us + china, which places Japan, Korea and Turkiye outside the OECD leg - so it is an OECD-core aggregate, not OECD-38.\nWEAKEST LINK (Jones/Baumol): the binding non-substitutable input is grid interconnection - HV cable, large power transformers, and licensed electricians - but with a twist specific to this effort: the shadow price is set by the DISCOUNT RATE on the physical asset, not by the engineering. A 40-year transmission asset financed at a 2.0-2.5% real risk-free plus a 150-250 bps spread has a levelized cost 35-45% above the same asset at 2015-19 real rates (assumptions: 40-yr life, 92% availability, flat real opex, single refinancing at year 20, no residual value credited). By 2036 the interconnection queue still caps realized datacenter load, which is why I put the realized AI-Q3 quantity near the locked ensemble's P10-P25 (600-800 TWh) rather than its P50. The SECOND weakest link, care labour hours, has a rising shadow price that is NOT PAID, because it is publicly funded under a tightening fiscal constraint; it clears by volume rationing. Never \"the Fermi math proves\" anything here - every line above rests on stated assumptions about asset life, utilization, refinancing and discount rate, and the alpha/beta/gamma shares are contested.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:57.711Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"US 10y TIPS real yield (FRED DFII10) monthly average prints below 1.60% for three consecutive months, WITHOUT an accompanying MSCI World drawdown of >= 20% in the same window (i.e. a non-flight-to-quality decline).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My explicit no-mean-reversion assumption (US 10y real averaging ~2.50% over 2032-36 against 2.60% observed Oct 30 2026) is wrong, and the secular forces I sized are cyclical after all. CQ4 P50 falls from 2.00% to ~1.50% and P10 to ~0.70%; CQ5 falls ~8 pp (a compressed term premium restores the equity-bond hedge, killing conjunct 2b); CQ6-D rises ~6 pp at B's expense (lower real rates with no growth payoff is the demand-deficit cell); CQ3 P50 rises only ~0.1 pp, since cheaper capital helps the hurdle-rate channel but does nothing for the hours or China-drag lines.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"An explicit curve-management or captive-demand measure announced by any of the US, UK, Germany, France or Italy: a statutory or central-bank yield cap, a formal YCC band, or a new regulatory holding mandate covering >= 5% of that sovereign's outstanding marketable stock.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My 45% repression probability is realized rather than optional, and the 2.4 pp Debt-at-Risk sensitivity to an AI equity shock has bound. CQ4 P50 falls ~35 bps to ~1.65% and the distribution's right tail compresses (P90 to ~2.5%); CQ5 falls ~7 pp (a pinned curve cannot deliver MOVE >= 105 and restores the drawdown hedge); CQ2 RISES ~5 pp, because repression is paid for in realized inflation and the OECD core conjunct is the one that gains; CQ6-C rises ~4 pp at B's expense.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD-38 harmonized unemployment prints <= 5.0% in every quarter from 2029Q1 through 2031Q4 inclusive (12 consecutive quarters, no single-quarter exception).\",\"by_when\":\"Mar 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My recession add-on arithmetic (+0.50 ordinary, +0.42 deep-tail, giving a 5.72% window mean) is too pessimistic and the demographic U-lowering term is larger than the -0.3 pp I assigned. CQ1 rises from 16 to ~26 (conjunct 1 to ~58%, conjunct 2 unchanged at 22% since the fiscal-monopsony obstacle is untouched by labour tightness); CQ3 P50 rises ~0.2 pp on hours; CQ6-A rises ~4 pp. Note this signpost moves ONLY the first conjunct - it is diagnostic of my labour-market arithmetic, not of my wage arithmetic.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Reported OECD-38 + China strict operational data-centre electricity consumption for calendar year 2032 comes in at >= 900 TWh (against ~365 TWh in 2024), on a strict-operational basis comparable to the AI-Q3 definition.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2033 (reporting lag allowed)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My cost-of-capital brake on AI capex is not binding, the realized AI-Q3 quantity belongs near its locked P50 of 998 rather than the P10-P25 range of 600-800 that I argued for, and the adapter's model-versus-lock datacenter disagreement resolves in the model's favour rather than mine. CQ3 P50 rises ~0.25 pp (capital deepening and TFP both); CQ6-A rises ~5 pp; CQ4 P50 rises ~20 bps, since the AI/grid capex claim on loanable funds that I haircut from +25 to +10 bps would be restored; CQ5 falls ~3 pp (a realized build means less impairment, so a smaller 2a).\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Real median hourly compensation in ISIC Q (health and social work) for the OECD-38 aggregate exceeds its 2025 level by >= 8% deflated by local CPI, on a published harmonized basis.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My fiscal-monopsony claim - that care's shadow price rises but is not paid, and that the adjustment is volume rationing - has failed, and public employers have in fact outbid debt service. CQ1 rises from 16 to ~30 (conjunct 2 roughly doubles to ~42%); plank (a) rises ~10 to ~17 (the pay side of the transition works even if the matching side does not); CQ6-B rises ~5 pp at C's and D's expense (broad-based physical-sector wage gains with contained inflation is the barbell's benign leg); CQ2's OECD core conjunct rises ~4 pp, since an 8% real care-wage award at Q's ~10-11% GVA share is itself a ~0.3-0.4 pp cost-push impulse.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 1 fermi seat, effort 'rstar'."}}